## Migration Analysis

**Three patterns found in your code:**

| Location | Pattern | Problem on .NET 8 |
|---|---|---|
| `Stop()` → `_thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | `SYSLIB0046` (obsolete in .NET 9, unreliable in .NET 8) |
| `Stop()` → `_thread.Abort()` | Cancellable work loop fallback | `PlatformNotSupportedException` — **crashes at runtime** |
| `RunLoop()` → `while (true)` + `WaitOne()` | No cooperative exit | Thread can never be stopped cleanly |

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _newEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new Queue<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new object();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        var handles = new WaitHandle[] { _newEvent, _cts.Token.WaitHandle };

        while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Blocks until either a new event arrives OR cancellation is requested
            int signaled = WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles);

            if (signaled == 1) // CancellationToken.WaitHandle fired
                break;

            _newEvent.Reset();

            string? item = null;
            lock (_lock)
            {
                if (_queue.Count > 0)
                    item = _queue.Dequeue();
            }

            if (item is not null)
                Handle(item);
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();          // wakes RunLoop via WaitHandle.WaitAny
        _thread?.Join(5000);    // wait up to 5s for graceful exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

## Key Changes Explained

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(handles)` | Allows cancellation to wake the blocked thread — `WaitOne` alone ignores cancellation |
| `while (true)` | `while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)` | Cooperative exit — the loop checks the token each iteration |
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Signals the `WaitHandle` in `WaitAny`, no exception needed |
| `_thread.Abort()` | *(removed)* | `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 8. Cooperative cancellation makes it unnecessary |
| No `Dispose` | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` and `ManualResetEvent` leak timers/handles if not disposed |

---

## Pitfalls to Watch For

1. **Race condition on `Dequeue`** — Your original code calls `_queue.Dequeue()` without checking `Count`. If multiple items are enqueued rapidly, `Set()` can coalesce and you dequeue once but miss items. The migrated version adds a `Count > 0` guard. Consider draining the queue in a loop.

2. **`Handle()` blocks cancellation** — If `Handle(item)` is long-running, the thread won't exit until it returns. Pass `_cts.Token` into `Handle()` and check it at safe points inside.

3. **Don't forget `Dispose`** — Leaking a `CancellationTokenSource` with registered callbacks or timers causes memory leaks. Always wrap in `using` or call `Dispose`.

4. **`WaitAny` index matters** — Put `_cts.Token.WaitHandle` at a consistent index (here index 1) and always check it. If you reorder the array, update the index check.
